Let the no-cURL build compile

q3rally's own additions to ioquake3 - the ladder logger and the version check -
have never been built with `USE_CURL` off. Each defines a helper inside its
`#ifdef USE_CURL` block and then calls it from outside:

  sv_ladder.c:1833   SV_LadderLogSubmitSnapshot  defined inside #ifdef USE_CURL
                     (opened at :1558), called at :2531 at conditional depth 0
  cl_update.c:102    CL_UpdateVersionCheck_Fail  defined inside #ifdef USE_CURL
                     (opened at :61), called at :244 inside #ifndef USE_CURL -
                     the branch whose whole job is to report "cURL support not
                     compiled"

Neither is a portability problem; both are ordinary build breaks on a
configuration upstream ships a switch for.

Each is fixed the way its own intent points, rather than uniformly:

**`sv_ladder.c` guards the call.** Without cURL there is no transport to submit a
snapshot over, so there is nothing to do and nothing to report.

**`cl_update.c` moves the helper out of the block.** The !USE_CURL path needs it
to say why the check is unavailable, and it depends only on setters that are
already outside the guard. Its one cURL-specific line, the handle cleanup, stays
guarded in place.

